# Overleaf compiles from the project root, and TeX does not search
# subdirectories for .sty or .bst files. The conference author kit is kept in
# style/iclr2027 unmodified -- that is the point of it, and flattening it into
# the root to satisfy a search path would make the kit indistinguishable from
# our own files.
#
# `ledger paper` sets the same two variables for the local build. Setting them
# here as well is what makes the project compile identically in a browser, with
# nothing about the layout changed to suit either one.
#
# The trailing `//` is a TeX path convention meaning "and every directory below".
$ENV{'TEXINPUTS'} = './style/iclr2027//:./generated//:./cases//:' . ($ENV{'TEXINPUTS'} // '');
$ENV{'BSTINPUTS'} = './style/iclr2027//:' . ($ENV{'BSTINPUTS'} // '');
$ENV{'BIBINPUTS'} = './:' . ($ENV{'BIBINPUTS'} // '');

# pdflatex, matching the local build. The document uses pgfplots and included
# PNGs, both of which pdflatex handles; xelatex would also work and is what to
# switch to if the native-script titles are ever typeset rather than rasterised.
$pdf_mode = 1;
